// Receives, by shape.
//
// `recv_start`'s `blocking` column is the whole discriminator between a
// receive that can hang and one that cannot: no `after` means no way
// out if the message never comes. Rules ask one of three questions and
// this file names them, so the column's encoding stays here.
//
// Requires imports.dl.

.decl receives(func: symbol)

receives(func) :- recv_start(_, func, _, _).

// No `after`: waits until the message arrives.
.decl blocking_receive(func: symbol)

blocking_receive(func) :- recv_start(_, func, 1, _).

// With an `after`: can end without the message.
.decl timed_receive(func: symbol)

timed_receive(func) :- recv_start(_, func, 0, _).

// A receive in the module has a clause for this literal message, or
// one that matches anything ("any").
.decl receives_message(mod: symbol, message: symbol)

receives_message(mod, message) :-
  recv_pattern(_, f, message),
  function_def(f, mod, _, _, _).

// ── A wait its peer ends ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// A receive with no `after` can still end no later than another process:
// when a clause takes that process's exit signal, whatever the reason it
// exits with, the wait lasts as long as the process lives. It is bounded
// by its peer, not by time — a peer that is alive and never answers
// holds it as long as it lives — and startup and blocking report such a
// wait as "down", apart from one nothing ends. Two shapes:
//
// - down_bounded(recv): a clause takes the `:DOWN` of a monitor: one the
//   receive's own function took, with no demonitor on the path to the
//   receive (recv_down), or one whose ref the clause pins wherever it came
//   from (recv_signal "down": proc_lib's await_DOWN(pid, ref), a
//   spawn_monitor's pair, gen_server's multi_call alias), with no
//   demonitor on any path from the function's entry to the receive: a
//   demonitor there may have removed the very monitor, and its :DOWN
//   never comes. The ref's monitor is taken on trust to be in place when
//   the function is entered. The runtime delivers the :DOWN once the
//   process exits, or at once if it is already gone.
// - exit_pinned(recv, func): a clause takes the `:EXIT` of the process
//   or port its sender pin names (recv_signal "exit"), in a function
//   that links (links_in). It ends the wait only in a process that traps
//   exits (in one that does not, the exit kills the waiter or never
//   comes), and trapping is the process's, not the function's: the
//   reader asks it (startup: init/1's own stack; blocking: the server's).
//
// mailbox asks another question of a monitor: whether it is released on
// every way out of the function (monitor_released, below), not whether a
// wait ends.
.decl down_bounded(recv: symbol)

down_bounded(id) :- recv_down(id, _, _).
down_bounded(id) :- recv_signal(id, _, "down").

.decl exit_pinned(recv: symbol, func: symbol)

exit_pinned(id, f) :- recv_signal(id, f, "exit"), links_in(f).

// The function makes a link its receive can be told of: a spawn_link, a
// link, a port (linked to the process that opens it), a start_link.
.decl links_in(func: symbol)

links_in(f) :- spawn_call(_, f, _, _, _, "spawn_link", _, _, _, _).
links_in(f) :- remote_call(_, f, ":erlang", "link", 1).
links_in(f) :- remote_call(_, f, "Process", "link", 1).
links_in(f) :- remote_call(_, f, ":erlang", "open_port", 2).
links_in(f) :- remote_call(_, f, "Port", "open", 2).
links_in(f) :- call_site(_, f, _, "start_link", _).

// ── A monitor released before its function returns ──────────────────
//
// monitor_released(monitor): every path from the monitor call to its
// function's return releases it (monitor_released_after of the monitor
// call itself, Argus.Extractors.Monitor): a receive clause that takes its
// :DOWN (any monitor's, or the one this call's ref pins), a demonitor of
// the ref, with or without :flush, or a call to a function of the module
// that waits so on every path. The clause, not the receive: the other
// clauses of `receive do {:reply, x} -> ...; {:DOWN, ...} -> ... end`
// leave it with the monitor live, and so does a timed receive's `after`.
// Unlike down_bounded, which asks whether a wait ENDS no later than its
// peer, this asks whether the monitor is RELEASED on every way out.
.decl monitor_released(monitor: symbol)

monitor_released(m) :- monitor_call(m, f, _), monitor_released_after(f, m).
